  "title": "Learning from label proportions for a visual matrix speller (ERP)",
  "description": "EEG recordings from 13 healthy participants performing an online copy-spelling task with a visual 6x7 matrix ERP speller. The speller was designed for a learning from label proportions (LLP) classifier, an unsupervised, calibration-free approach that reconstructs target and non-target responses from known class proportions across stimulus sequences. The dataset supports evaluation of calibrationless P300-based brain-computer interfaces, with 31 EEG channels recorded at 1000 Hz.",
  "methods_description": "Participants copy-spelled a German pangram three times using a visual ERP speller with a 6x7 grid and a 250 ms stimulus onset asynchrony. Two stimulus sequences with different target/non-target ratios (3/8 and 2/18) were used, giving 68 highlighting events per character. EEG was recorded with a BrainAmp DC amplifier and 31 passive Ag/AgCl electrodes (EasyCap), nose reference, at 1000 Hz. The LLP classifier was reset at the start of each sentence and retrained after each character.",
